grade the copy out with neon

With blue light on the shelf, its menu and the cart animations still ran
at 30 fps on 0050, and with it off they did not. Every frame through the
canvas pays the copy to the panel, and on 0050 that took 9.3 ms with blue
light on, every frame of the trace, against 2.7 with it off on 7843ad0:
the difference between a 10 ms shelf frame and a 17 ms one. 0049 had
moved that copy's writes to a row of RAM on the theory that byte stores to
the uncached panel were the cost; they were not, it stayed at 9.3. The
cost is the grading itself, three table lookups a pixel.

LLVM leaves even a plain fixed-point multiply over the row scalar -- one
mul and two byte moves a byte, checked in the armv7 disassembly -- so the
row is graded by a NEON loop in inline asm, as `scale_row` is: sixteen
bytes loaded, widened, multiplied by the gains, narrowed by eight bits,
stored. The gain table is now the same 8.8 the game pass carries in its
column mask, `(v * g) >> 8`, so the canvas, the surround and the picture
are graded alike, and the loop's bytes are the table's.

Not yet measured on the Flip.

Tests, as armv7 under qemu: the NEON grade matches the scalar reference
and the gain table at every blue light step, for rows that end mid-chunk;
it writes nothing past the end. Narrowing by 7 instead of 8 fails it.

Applies on top of 0050, upstream 91499a9.

